A||N||15||jhogans||Windshield Wiper Problems||||5||
Z||000000||jhogans||12-14-1999||02:56 AM||||I tried to use my wipers today but they would not come on.  <P>What are my next steps to figuring out what the problem is?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||04:04 AM||||Check the fuse.  If that's good, check for power at the wiper motor.  If you have power at the fuse box but not at the wiper motor, then your problem is some where inbetween.  Did anything change since the last time you used them?  (Downpour, mods, etc.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhogans||01-04-2000||05:49 PM||||I have never used my wipers since I bought the car.  I should have checked them before I bought it but I overlooked that issue.<P>Monkeyman, I checked the fuse and there was no fuse.  so I put one in and the wiper came on.  YEEEHAAAAWWWWW!!!!!  But wait, they wouldn't turn off.  Now I see why there was no fuse.<P>What is the next step for me?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||01-04-2000||05:59 PM||||It could be in your switch, maybe a short in the motor, or it could be in the ?relay? inside your steering column.  These are just guesses at this point.  I'd check the 1st 2.  They should be the easiest.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jhogans||01-04-2000||06:57 PM||||Thanks.  I will check it out.  I ran a couple of tests just a while ago.  Here is the symptoms.<P>Off - The wipers are on with the same speed as the HI mode.<P>Delay - This works as it should<P>Lo - This works as it should.  It moves at a slow pace<P>Hi - This works as it should.  It moves at a little faster pace.||||reg||1
Z||000005||daved||01-04-2000||09:23 PM||||Well Haynes is no help, and neither are the Mitchell International schematics I have. It appears there is an electronic control unit somewhere (under the dash, or actually part of the motor unit itself?), anyone know?  Find that, and I think you will find the culprit. If I had a cicuit diagram I could provide further help, sorry, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||01-04-2000||09:45 PM||||I believe the ECU is inside the steering column somewhere.  I've never had to worry about it so I'm not positive about this.  Search this topic and you'll come up with a few other threads that might help.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ka4nkf||01-04-2000||11:31 PM||||Boilermaker told me that the ECU is located inside the wiper motor, and it does give trouble. You might give boilermaker a call and talk with him, as he changed his out i think. I dont think it is a big job. I need to change mine but just havent gotten around to it. I pulled my fuse until i can get it fixed.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ed||01-04-2000||11:35 PM||||There's (at least) two failure spots. The stalk off the steering column, and the electronics inside the wiper motor unit. If the electronics inside the motor unit get wet, for example, they will cause your wipers to wipe. The module could fail for other reasons too.   ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phaeton||01-05-2000||12:24 AM||||There is a park switch that controls the wiper motor so the wipers stop in the correct spot.  If this switch fails then the wipers run continuous when the main switch is turned off.  Dealer part but easy to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GTBro||01-05-2000||01:06 AM||||I also have a problem with my wipers.  When the car is on, they only operate in the high position.  Also, when I turn the car on, the wipers annoyingly park in the middle of the windshield right in my line of vision.<P>Could this be caused by the same thing?||||reg||5
Z||000011||Phaeton||01-05-2000||04:09 AM||||Probably not the park switch if other controls don't function proper, park switch just sits its life away waiting for the juice to go off so it can put the wipers in their place.  When your wipers stop in the middle of your windshild does the lever still work to turn them on and off?  If it takes awhile to get them on the lever is likely at fault and is tripping the breaker, specially since only one speed works.  But then it could be the park switch twisted around, the switch is in the box on the end of the motor and is relatively easy to work on compared to the column lever.  And while the motor is off it can have it's bearings checked-just in case.  But you said the wipers stop in the middle when you turn the car on, if they go back to the correct spot later then the switches in the motor are OK.  Trying to beat a stubborn Fiero is much more fun than gaming.<p>[This message has been edited by Phaeton (edited 01-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||862M4inCA||01-06-2000||01:11 PM||||I have seen the circuit board that you are refering to inside a rectangular cover plate on the motor. Mine was held in with a fladhead screw. I pulled mine out after checking my wipers out. I had a problem that when in the off and hi position the wipers ran, however in the delay and low the wipers were off. I pulled the board and found that a diode had burnt up. I went to the boneyard and pulled a "replacement" and put it in. All worked fine for about 10 minutes (including the phantom wiper syndrome, hehe) after that the wipers wouldn't turn off in any position. I pulled the board out again and found that a trace on the board had burnt out. it was the small trace on the board coming from the relay located in the lower right corner. Any suggestions on what to check to prevent this from happening again when I do decide to go to the dealer and pay the $75 for a new board?? Any help is greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000013||burdell1||01-08-2000||03:55 PM||||i have the exact same problem. I noticed that when I replace the old circuit board with a different one, the wires on the wiper switch get very hot. 862M4inCA, did you replace your wiper switch with a used one before this happened? I did and that is when the trouble started. Although I am still looking for a cheap, but new replacement wiper switch....||||reg||1
Z||000014||862M4inCA||01-09-2000||02:27 PM||||Well I can't say I have replaced my wiper switch in the steering column since I don't care to spend all day trying to pull everything out to find out that it wasn't my switch after all. However I did notice that the wires going to the circuit board did not get warm/hot. I'm just wondering if maybe the board itself was bad. Anyone else have any input on this? ||||reg||1
Z||000015||burdell1||05-05-2000||02:23 PM||||when I examined the wiring going from the wiper motor to the wiring harness (on the firewall) I noticed only the purple wire would get really hot. Does anyone know what the particular wire does in relation to the wipers?||||reg||5
